
<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://i3wm.org/">i3</a> is simple. i3 is fast.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A window manager for <strong>keyboard-oriented</strong> workflows.<br>It doesn&#8217;t substitute your desktop environment.<br>It doesn&#8217;t want to be your new GNOME or KDE.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It just helps to build a workflow. For speed. With clarity.</p>



<span id="more-9485"></span>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">The Basic Tools</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">As i3 is simple, you have to take care of your tools.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Here is a curated <strong>Ghost Operations</strong> list of the useful applications:</p>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table class="has-fixed-layout"><thead><tr><th class="has-text-align-center" data-align="center">Name</th><th class="has-text-align-center" data-align="center">Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">alacritty</td><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">Terminal emulator</td></tr><tr><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">rofi</td><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">App switcher</td></tr><tr><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">thunar</td><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">File manager</td></tr><tr><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">dunst</td><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">Notification daemon</td></tr><tr><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">copyq</td><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">Clipboard manager</td></tr><tr><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">flameshot</td><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">Screenshot software</td></tr><tr><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">pavucontrol</td><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">Volume control</td></tr><tr><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">arandr</td><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">Screen &amp; display manager</td></tr><tr><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">lxappearance</td><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">GTK theme switcher</td></tr><tr><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">feh</td><td class="has-text-align-left" data-align="left">Image viewer</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Install them:</p>



<pre class="wp-block-preformatted">sudo apt install \
     i3 \
     i3lock \
     alacritty \
     rofi \
     thunar \
     dunst \
     copyq \
     flameshot \
     pavucontrol \
     arandr \
     lxappearance \
     feh
</pre>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Basic Configuration</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Start the most important tools and daemons.<br>Set up the wallpaper.</p>



<pre class="wp-block-preformatted">bindsym $mod+Return exec alacritty
bindsym $mod+d exec rofi -show drun
bindsym $mod+x exec i3lock -u -c 1c202e
exec --no-startup-id dunst
bindsym Print exec flameshot gui
exec_always --no-startup-id feh --bg-fill ~/Pictures/wallpaper.jpg

# Border style
hide_edge_borders smart
default_border normal 1
</pre>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Adjust The Display Scaling</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">To set up the proper display scaling use the <code>Xresources</code>.</p>



<pre class="wp-block-preformatted">vim ~/.Xresources
</pre>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Add the configuration to this file:</p>



<pre class="wp-block-preformatted">Xft.dpi: 120
Xft.antialias: true
Xft.hinting: true
Xft.rgba: rgb
</pre>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In the <code>i3/config</code> enable the configuration:</p>



<pre class="wp-block-preformatted">exec --no-startup-id xrandr --output DP-0 --mode 2560x1440 --scale 1x1
exec --no-startup-id xrdb -merge ~/.Xresources
</pre>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <strong>output name</strong> and <strong>resolution</strong> depend on your hardware.<br><code>Xft.dpi</code> affects applications that honor this resource; it does not uniformly scale every application.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Sometimes the screen &#8220;tears&#8221; while scrolling.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You can install <code>picom</code>, a lightweight compositor:</p>



<pre class="wp-block-preformatted">sudo apt install picom
</pre>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Create the config:</p>



<pre class="wp-block-preformatted">mkdir -p ~/.config/picom
vim ~/.config/picom/picom.conf
</pre>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Add:</p>



<pre class="wp-block-preformatted">backend = "glx";
vsync = true;
</pre>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Add it to the i3 config:</p>



<pre class="wp-block-preformatted">exec --no-startup-id picom
</pre>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Configure The Status Bar</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Move the global configuration to your local config and set it up for your taste.</p>



<pre class="wp-block-preformatted">mkdir -p ~/.config/i3status
cp /etc/i3status.conf ~/.config/i3status/config
</pre>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><code>i3status</code> generates the status information, while <code>i3bar</code> displays it.<br>The bar block in your i3 configuration controls the status command.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Multiple Display Setup &#8211; ARandR or XRandR</h3>



<pre class="wp-block-preformatted">xrandr --output HDMI-1 --primary --mode 1920x1080 \
       --output DP-1 --mode 1920x1080 --right-of HDMI-1
</pre>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Or use <code>arandr</code> and save the presets.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Workspace Configuration</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Inspect window properties and classes with <code>xprop</code>:</p>



<pre class="wp-block-preformatted">xprop
</pre>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Assign classes to workspaces:</p>



<pre class="wp-block-preformatted">set $workspace1 "1: Terminals"
assign [class="Alacritty"] $workspace1
</pre>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Audio &#8211; PipeWire</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Install the PipeWire audio components:</p>



<pre class="wp-block-preformatted">sudo apt install pipewire pipewire-pulse wireplumber
</pre>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">NetworkManager</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Install the network manager tool to manage your connection in i3:</p>



<pre class="wp-block-preformatted">sudo apt install network-manager network-manager-gnome
</pre>



<pre class="wp-block-preformatted">exec --no-startup-id nm-applet
</pre>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Final Thoughts</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">i3 doesn&#8217;t want to be your GNOME or KDE.<br>i3 is like a nice, modular tool you can build up.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It&#8217;s for keyboard-based workflows.<br>Lean. Fast.</p>
